% We observe similar trends across other platforms in Appendix Figure~\ref{fig:topic_web_call_rate_over_time_all_platforms}.
%

\if 0
To disentangle these effects, Figure~\ref{fig:prompts_and_web_call_per_topic} compares, for a set of topics, the temporal evolution of user prompt share with the corresponding Web-calling rate. The two trends do not evolve uniformly across topics. For example, topics such as \textit{Health} exhibit substantial growth in user activity but only a modest increase in Web-search usage, suggesting that conversational agents continue to rely primarily on their parametric knowledge. In contrast, topics such as \textit{Travel} show comparatively smaller changes in user demand but a much steeper increase in Web-calling rates, indicating an increasing reliance on Web search. Overall, these observations suggest that the long-term increase in Web-search usage is driven jointly by evolving user workloads and an increasing tendency of conversational agents to invoke Web search for particular topics.
\fi

