%Table~\ref{tab:replay} first shows that disabling Web search for prompts that autonomously called Web search \textit{consistently degrades response quality} across all three metrics. 
Table~\ref{tab:replay} first shows that disabling Web search generally degrades response quality for prompts that autonomously invoked Web search, although the magnitude and direction of the effect vary across models and evaluation metrics. Overall, Web search is most beneficial for GPT-5.3-chat, Grok-4.3, and DeepSeek-v4-flash, while Claude exhibits mixed effects. 
Paired bootstrap significance tests confirm that nearly all observed differences are statistically significant ($p < 10^{-3}$).
In contrast, prompts for which models do not invoke Web search achieve comparatively higher quality, suggesting that these requests can often be answered from parametric knowledge alone.

However, a larger number of Web calls does not necessarily indicate better response quality. Although Claude, Grok, and DeepSeek invoke Web search significantly more than GPT-5.3-chat (as seen in Table~\ref{tab:replay}), the resulting gains in factuality, completeness, and relevance remain modest. Note that our findings do not suggest that one platform's Web-search is inherently better than the other's. Instead, they indicate that increasing the number of Web calls does not necessarily yield better response quality. This calls for more principled Web-search decision mechanisms that invoke Web search only when it provides meaningful benefit.

