\section{Analyzing Search Responses}
\label{sec:assembly_model_responses}

%Once conversational agents are fed with information from the Web, they must decide which sources to trust, which of them to expose as citations, and how to combine the search results with their own parametric knowledge. 
%In this section, we first investigate how conversational agents select citations from the search results, and then study how these citations support individual response claims.

We now analyze how conversational agents synthesize the final response for user prompts using the returned search results from different sources together as well as their own parametric knowledge.
%
A characteristic feature of LLM agent responses involving Web search is the grounding of the claims by citing supporting external Web pages (URLs). 
%
To understand how search results are  incorporated into responses,
we decompose responses into atomic claims.
%
We first analyze how agents \textit{select / generate citations} from the search results and incorporate them into the responses,
and then investigate the extent to which the atomic claims from the response are \textit{grounded} in cited and retrieved sources.


