\section{Analyzing Search Queries and Results}
%\section{Evolution of user prompts into actionable Web queries}
\label{sec:Webqueries}

\subsection{Querying strategies}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item In {\tt in vivo} setup, we find that LLM agents rewrite a single user prompt as both {\it multi-queries}, i.e., queries that are issued simultaneously and in parallel to cover different perspectives and {\it multi-hop queries}, i.e., queries that are issued iteratively and in sequence to build on results uncovered at each hop. ChatGPT, Grok, and DeepSeek all use both multi-queries and multi-hop queries, while Claude relies only on multi-hop queries that go lot deeper. 
    \item In {\tt in vitro} setup, we find that while ChatGPT mostly issues (2) multi-queries in a single-hop, Claude, Grok, and DeepSeek generate single queries across multi-hops.   
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Query formulation and reformulations}
All experimental results below are referring to {\tt in vivo} setup.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item User prompts are not repeated verbatim as queries -- rather LLMs formulate queries comprising of keywords that capture the essence of the prompt. The number of terms in web queries is far less than the terms in user prompts. However, they are still considerably more than queries issued by human users.
    \item A vast majority of the query terms are derived not only from the last user prompt that triggered the query, but also from the prompts preceding it, which are part of the same conversation. For multi-hop queries, query terms in later iterations are derived from search results returned in earlier iterations. Additionally, a few terms are added by the LLMs themselves. 
    \item For multi-hop queries, queries in the later iterations have additional terms that specify additional context by the way of location, time, entities, and numbers.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Bias in search results}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item number of results per query. number of results per call. Comparison across platforms.
    \item do the search engines across different platforms show biases towards certain domains.
\end{enumerate}


{\bf Take-away: Different LLMs Despite the differing costs and quality of searches following different strategies.} 

\section{Analyzing Search Responses}
\subsection{Selection of citations}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item A non-trivial fraction (10-30\%) of all search results are used for citations. Citations are biased towards result URLs that are popular and highly ranked. 
    \item Most citations are from search results, but a small fraction are from parametric knowledge. 
    \item Hallucinations occur in both types of cited URLs -- be they from search results or parametrically generated.
\end{enumerate}
\subsection{Grounding of claims}
We break the generated response into atomic claims. We check which of the claims are supported by retrieved search results.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item A significant fraction of all claims are properly attributed and associated with citations. 
    \item However, many claims are also supported by content fetched from returned URLs that are not cited at all -- potentially concerning from the perspective of attributing credit. 
    \item Unsupported claims, likely generated from parametric knowledge, are less factually accurate.
\end{enumerate}
